VALUES ('AS','published','Global sports and outdoor company whose brands include Arc''teryx, Salomon, Wilson, Atomic and Peak Performance.','Amer Sports designs and sells premium technical apparel, footwear, equipment and accessories through wholesale and direct-to-consumer channels. Brand heat, product innovation, Greater China exposure, inventory and leverage shape owner returns.','Outdoor apparel and footwear, winter-sports equipment, trail and running products, tennis and ball-sports equipment and related accessories.','Wholesale shipments to retailers plus owned stores and e-commerce sales, with economics varying by brand, geography, channel and season.','Premium brand expansion, direct-to-consumer growth, technical product innovation and international penetration can raise revenue and margin.','Amer Sports must preserve brand scarcity, forecast demand, manage inventory, expand stores prudently, control sourcing and logistics and reduce leverage without weakening innovation.','Consolidated growth can conceal brand and channel differences. Track Arc''teryx, Salomon and Wilson separately, then reconcile China, DTC, wholesale, currency, inventory and debt.','A portfolio of premium technical brands can compound globally','["Arc''teryx and other technical brands can support premium pricing.","Direct-to-consumer channels deepen customer data and gross margin.","A diversified brand portfolio spans outdoor, winter and ball sports."]','Fashion cycles, inventory and leverage raise downside','["Brand heat can fade and force discounting.","China and controlling-shareholder exposure add geographic and governance risk.","Inventory, store investment, currency and debt can weaken cash conversion."]','The thesis breaks if flagship-brand growth requires persistent discounting and working capital while leverage and minority-owner governance prevent per-share cash compounding','[{"label":"BRAND / CHANNEL GROWTH","value":"Demand quality","detail":"Brand sales, comparable stores, e-commerce, wholesale, geography and full-price mix"},{"label":"INVENTORY / MARGIN","value":"Execution","detail":"Inventory growth, markdowns, sourcing, freight, gross margin and returns"},{"label":"LEVERAGE / CASH","value":"Owner outcome","detail":"Operating cash flow, capital spending, leases, interest and net debt"}]','[{"title":"Brand and fashion","level":"High","text":"Demand can change quickly and premium positioning can erode.","signal":"Full-price sell-through, markdowns, traffic and brand concentration"},{"title":"China and governance","level":"High","text":"Regional concentration and controlling ownership create geopolitical and conflict risks.","signal":"China share, related parties, board rights and distributions"},{"title":"Inventory and leverage","level":"High","text":"Seasonality and store expansion can consume cash while debt raises fixed cost.","signal":"Inventory turns, free cash flow, leases and net leverage"}]','[{"title":"Brand-level durability","text":"Track full-price demand, repeat purchase and margin by major brand and region.","impact":"BULL CASE"},{"title":"Cash after growth","text":"Require DTC expansion and inventory investment to produce free cash flow and lower leverage.","impact":"NEUTRAL"}]','["How much growth comes from new space versus comparable demand?","Are inventory and markdowns rising faster than sales?","How are controlling-owner interests aligned with public shareholders?"]','Nike, adidas, Lululemon, VF Corporation, Deckers, Columbia Sportswear, Anta brands and specialist outdoor and sporting-goods companies.','2026-02-26','2026-08-05T01:07:00Z',CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP)
