VALUES ('ASIC','published','Specialty excess-and-surplus insurer serving small and medium-sized U.S. businesses.','Ategrity uses a technology-supported underwriting platform and wholesale distribution to write E&S commercial risks. Premium growth creates value only if pricing exceeds claims, expenses and reinsurance cost while reserves remain conservative.','Excess-and-surplus commercial insurance products distributed through wholesale brokers.','Earned insurance premiums net of reinsurance, underwriting expenses and claims, plus investment income on the insurance portfolio.','Standard carriers often avoid complex small-business risks, creating room for disciplined specialty pricing and efficient low-touch underwriting.','Ategrity must price risk accurately, control catastrophe and concentration exposure, maintain reserves, renew reinsurance and scale distribution without relaxing underwriting standards.','Premium growth is not profit. Track current accident-year loss ratio, prior-year development, expense ratio, combined ratio, retention, catastrophe exposure, reinsurance and tangible book value.','Technology can improve small-account specialty underwriting','["E&S flexibility permits tailored terms and risk-based pricing.","Automated workflows can lower handling cost for smaller accounts.","Wholesale relationships and data can broaden profitable submissions."]','Reserve and catastrophe surprises can erase years of gains','["Fast growth can conceal weaker underwriting vintages.","Claims inflation and catastrophes can exceed pricing assumptions.","Reinsurance can become more expensive or less available."]','The thesis breaks if growth produces adverse reserve development and a through-cycle combined ratio that fails to compound tangible book value per share','[{"label":"PREMIUM / RETENTION","value":"Growth quality","detail":"Gross and net written premium, policies, renewal price, retention and mix"},{"label":"COMBINED RATIO","value":"Underwriting proof","detail":"Loss, expense and catastrophe ratios plus prior-year development"},{"label":"CAPITAL / REINSURANCE","value":"Resilience","detail":"Ceded structure, limits, investment risk and tangible book value"}]','[{"title":"Reserves and claims inflation","level":"High","text":"Ultimate losses may exceed initial estimates.","signal":"Prior-year development, reserve changes and paid-to-incurred trends"},{"title":"Catastrophe and concentration","level":"High","text":"Weather or correlated losses can exceed modeled expectations.","signal":"Exposure by peril and region, event losses and limits"},{"title":"Reinsurance and growth","level":"High","text":"Capacity can reprice while rapid growth weakens selection.","signal":"Ceded cost, retention, renewal terms and vintage loss ratios"}]','[{"title":"Underwriting vintages","text":"Track loss and reserve development by accident year as premium scales.","impact":"BULL CASE"},{"title":"Book-value compounding","text":"Require underwriting and investment earnings to increase tangible book value through catastrophe cycles.","impact":"NEUTRAL"}]','["Does automated underwriting improve loss selection or mainly processing speed?","How conservative are reserves for the newest fast-growing vintages?","What reinsurance cost and retention apply under a severe catastrophe year?"]','Kinsale Capital, RLI, Skyward Specialty, Palomar, James River and other E&S insurers.','2026-03-06','2026-08-05T01:07:00Z',CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP)
