VALUES ('ASLE','published','Aviation aftermarket company selling and leasing aircraft, engines and components while providing MRO and engineered solutions.','AerSale acquires aviation assets, monetizes used serviceable material, leases or sells equipment and performs maintenance, repair, overhaul and engineering work. Asset purchase discipline, inventory turns, shop utilization and certification determine cash returns.','Used aircraft, engines and components; leasing and asset management; MRO; engineered products and aviation technology solutions.','Asset sales and leases, component and material sales, maintenance and repair fees and engineered-product revenue.','Aging fleets, parts scarcity, airline maintenance demand and certified efficiency or safety products can expand aftermarket revenue.','AerSale must buy assets below realizable value, harvest parts efficiently, keep shops utilized, earn regulatory approvals, manage customer credit and convert inventory into cash.','Accounting profit depends on asset assumptions. Track inventory aging, part-out timing, lease collections, maintenance backlog, shop utilization, certification milestones and cash invested per asset package.','Integrated asset and MRO capabilities can capture aftermarket value','["Aircraft and engine expertise can improve acquisition and part-out decisions.","MRO and material operations can cross-sell across an asset life cycle.","Certified engineered solutions can add differentiated higher-margin revenue."]','Inventory, cycle and certification risk can trap capital','["Aircraft and engine values can fall with fleet or traffic changes.","Large asset purchases may take years to monetize.","Certification delays, customer concentration and shop execution can defer cash."]','The thesis breaks if asset acquisitions and engineered programs consume cash without acceptable inventory turns, certification success and through-cycle returns','[{"label":"ASSET MONETIZATION","value":"Trading engine","detail":"Purchases, sales, leases, part-outs, inventory age and gross profit"},{"label":"MRO / BACKLOG","value":"Service engine","detail":"Shop visits, utilization, labor, turnaround time, backlog and margin"},{"label":"ENGINEERED SOLUTIONS","value":"Differentiation","detail":"Certification, installations, orders, reliability and program cash"}]','[{"title":"Asset and inventory","level":"High","text":"Used aviation values and part demand can change before inventory is sold.","signal":"Inventory days, impairments, fleet retirements and cash conversion"},{"title":"Certification and product","level":"High","text":"Regulatory approval or customer adoption can take longer than expected.","signal":"FAA milestones, supplemental type certificates, orders and installations"},{"title":"Aviation and execution cycle","level":"Medium","text":"Airline health, maintenance timing and skilled labor affect demand and delivery.","signal":"Backlog, shop utilization, turnaround, receivables and customer concentration"}]','[{"title":"Cash by asset cohort","text":"Track purchase cash, monetization timing and realized return for aircraft and engine packages.","impact":"NEUTRAL"},{"title":"Certified product adoption","text":"Require regulatory milestones to convert into installed units, recurring demand and cash margin.","impact":"BULL CASE"}]','["What realized cash return is earned on recent asset packages?","How much backlog is firm and executable with current labor and capacity?","Which certifications and installations create repeatable product economics?"]','AAR, Air Transport Services Group, FTAI Aviation, HEICO, StandardAero, independent MROs and aviation asset traders.','2026-03-10','2026-08-05T01:07:00Z',CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP)
